# The math anchor's substrate: truncations of the Rieger–Nishimura lattice
(math anchor, Phases 2–4; pre-registered in `validation/claims/math-anchor-cantor-floor.md`)

The free Heyting algebra on one generator `F(1)` is the Rieger–Nishimura
lattice (Rieger 1957; Nishimura 1960).  It is infinite, so the four-position
forced-kernel experiment cannot be run on it by `decide`.  The **canonical
finite truncations** are the finite one-generated Heyting algebras: by
Citkin's algebraic proof of the Nishimura theorem (*Logics* 2(4), 2024,
Theorems 6 & 8), **for every `n > 1` there is exactly one one-generated
Heyting algebra `Z_n` of cardinality `n`** (up to isomorphism), namely the
algebra defined by the relation `x_n = 1` in the Nishimura term sequence

```
x_0 = g ⊓ ¬g (= ⊥),  x_1 = ¬g,  x_2 = g,
x_{2n+3} = x_{2n+1} ⇨ x_{2n},   x_{2n+4} = x_{2n+1} ⊔ x_{2n+2}.
```

This *removes the truncation-arbitrariness risk pre-registered as the most
likely failure mode*: the truncation parameter is a single integer `n`, and
"stability across truncations" is a well-posed question.

## What this file proves (all by `decide`, all kernel-checked)

Each algebra below is certified as `Z_n` by two facts: it has cardinality `n`
(`Fintype.card`), and every element is an explicit Heyting term in the
generator `g` (`*_one_generated`).  By Citkin's uniqueness theorem **[C]**
that pins it as *the* `Z_n`.  Then the forced-kernel experiment runs:

* **`Z_5` (and below): no kernel works** (`Z5.no_kernel`).  Every element is
  regular or has `⊥` complement, so some cell is always empty.  The
  four-position partition needs cardinality ≥ 6 to be non-degenerate.

* **`Z_6` *is* the music lattice.**  `Div12` — the divisor lattice of 12, the
  music anchor's substrate — is itself a 6-element one-generated Heyting
  algebra, generated by the **tritone**: every element of `Div12` is a
  Nishimura term in `two` (`Div12.one_generated_by_tritone`).  By Citkin's
  uniqueness theorem [C], `Div12 ≅ Z_6`.  The music substrate was never
  music-specific: it is the cardinality-6 truncation of the free Heyting
  algebra on one generator, and the tritone is the free generator.

* **`Z_7`, `Z_8`: unique forced kernel = the generator** (`Z7.kernel_unique`,
  `Z8.kernel_unique`), with the same witnesses in Nishimura terms.

* **Stability (pre-registered outcome A).**  Bundled as
  `rn_truncation_outcome_A`: at every checked truncation (`n = 5, 6, 7, 8`)
  the kernel at which all four cells are inhabited is **unique and equal to
  the free generator** (vacuously none for `n = 5`), and the four cells are
  inhabited by the *same* four terms at every `n ≥ 6`:

  | Cell           | Term        | in `Div12 = Z_6`            |
  |----------------|-------------|------------------------------|
  | Infrastructure | `g`         | tritone `⟨6⟩`               |
  | Refusal        | `¬g`        | augmented triad `⟨4⟩`       |
  | Exploitation   | `¬¬g`       | diminished seventh `⟨3⟩`    |
  | Distribution   | `g ⊔ ¬g`    | whole-tone hexachord `⟨2⟩`  |

  The Exploitation witness is literally `¬¬g` — the double-negation
  non-collapse `¬¬g ≠ g` of intuitionistic logic (`*_g_non_regular`).

## Pre-registration audit (against `math-anchor-cantor-floor.md`)

Outcomes pre-registered: (A) unique stable kernel; (B) multiple kernels;
(C) no kernel anywhere; (D) truncation-dependent kernel.  **Result: (A)** on
the checked range `n ∈ {5,6,7,8}` (with the sharpening that `n = 5` is
degenerate — below cardinality 6 there is no kernel at all).  The extension
to *all* `n` is proved abstractly in `NishimuraKernelLaw.lean`
(`nishimura_kernel_unique`): conditional only on the classical Nishimura
enumeration **[C]**, the unique four-cell kernel of *every* one-generated
Heyting algebra with ordinary generator — every `Z_n` with `n ≥ 6`, and the
full Rieger–Nishimura lattice itself — is the free generator.

## Interpretation honesty

The cells get *logical* names from the lattice's own structure: `g`, `¬g`,
`¬¬g`, `g ⊔ ¬g`.  This is intuitionistic propositional logic on **one**
generator — chosen as the *simplest* free Heyting algebra, not forced by
"mathematics".  No provability/Gödel content is claimed: the Gödel resonance
of the math anchor lives in the Cantor/Lawvere floor (`MathFloorCantor.lean`),
not in this lattice.

## Sources

* Citkin, A. (2024). An Algebraic Proof of the Nishimura Theorem.
  *Logics* 2(4), 148–157.  (Theorem 6: uniqueness of `Z_n`; Theorem 8:
  `x_n = 1` defines `Z_n`; Corollary 9: the infinite one-generated Heyting
  algebra is free.)
* Nishimura, I. (1960). On formulas of one variable in intuitionistic
  propositional calculus. *J. Symb. Log.* 25, 327–331.
* Bumpus, B. M. & Kocsis, Z. A. Degree of satisfiability in Heyting
  algebras.  (Rieger–Nishimura term recursion.)
